+// AOT tick-exactness (docs/aot-size-and-coverage.md item 12, NANOBOX_TICKEXACT, default 0).
+// AOT mode moves or removes the trace boundaries at which cpu_loop decides whether a timer is
+// due and whether an async event is pending, so the guest takes its interrupts at DIFFERENT
+// instruction boundaries than the reference -- which is why its tick count diverges. Each bit
+// restores one of those decision points; 0 (the default) is bit-for-bit today's AOT engine.
+//   1  self-loop back edge syncs every iteration (instead of every nanobox_jit_aot_tick)
+//   2  forward in-region edges take the full boundary (sync + async_event) like a back edge
+//   4  the fetch-window check is emitted on every in-region edge
+//   8  direct calls off (a guest CALL is a trace boundary in the interpreter; the dcall path
+//      enters the callee without one). Changes the shared wasm signature -- set before compiling.
+//  16  direct calls KEPT, with the boundary test folded into the site's validity chain: a site whose
+//      timer is due / async event is pending falls through to the ordinary exit. Same exactness as
+//      bit 8, ~5 wasm instructions per site instead of losing the 2.2x call+ret.
+//  32  self-loop window sized from the countdown instead of fixed at nanobox_jit_aot_tick: the sync
+//      still happens on the iteration the interpreter would have taken it, but the fast path keeps
+//      its 5-instruction countdown (bit 1's alternative, and much cheaper).
